[s0l0!-header][h1]Constructors[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

[b]Constructors [/b]are special methods invoked when an object is created and are used to initialize them. 
A constructor can be used to provide initial values for object attributes.

- A constructor name must be same as its class name.
- A constructor must have no explicit return type.

[s0l0!-header][b]Example of a constructor:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="java" ]public class Vehicle {
  private String color;
  [b]Vehicle() [/b]{
     color = "Red";
  }
}[/code]
The [b]Vehicle[/b]() method is the constructor of our class, so whenever an object of that class is created, the color attribute will be set to "Red".
A constructor can also take parameters to initialize attributes. [code format="java" ]public class Vehicle {
  private String color;
  [b]Vehicle(String c) [/b]{
    color = c;
  }
}[/code]
[note]You can think of constructors as methods that will set up your class by default, so you don’t need to repeat the same code every time.[/note]

